WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.418 --> 00:00:03.650
Kubernetes is a platform
for managing your containers.

00:00:03.650 --> 00:00:06.258
Machine learning workflows
can often get really messy,

00:00:06.258 --> 00:00:07.680
especially in production,

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:09.189
so it was only a matter of time

00:00:09.189 --> 00:00:11.559
before somebody put the two together.

00:00:11.769 --> 00:00:14.719
How do we do machine learning
on Kubernetes

00:00:14.719 --> 00:00:16.565
without too much extra work?

00:00:16.870 --> 00:00:18.458
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:26.652 --> 00:00:28.351
Welcome to <i>AI Adventures,</i>

00:00:28.351 --> 00:00:32.506
where we explore the art, science,
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:32.506 --> 00:00:34.256
My name is Yufeng Guo,

00:00:34.256 --> 00:00:37.150
and on this episode
we're going to check out Kubeflow,

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:39.466
an open-source project
to help make running

00:00:39.466 --> 00:00:43.170
machine learning training and prediction
on Kubernetes clusters easier.

00:00:43.454 --> 00:00:46.579
The Kubeflow project is dedicated
to making deployments

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:49.446
of machine learning workflows
on Kubernetes simple,

00:00:49.446 --> 00:00:51.139
portable, and scalable.

00:00:51.139 --> 00:00:55.189
More specifically, it wants to allow you
to easily deploy your code

00:00:55.189 --> 00:00:58.338
to all sorts of environments--
from, say, your laptop

00:00:58.338 --> 00:01:00.533
to a desktop with two GPUs,

00:01:00.533 --> 00:01:05.120
or, say, a training Kubernetes cluster
versus a production cluster.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:07.168
And, of course,
this wouldn't be Kubernetes

00:01:07.168 --> 00:01:10.500
if it didn't allow you
to scale everything with demand.

00:01:10.951 --> 00:01:12.443
Kubeflow began life

00:01:12.443 --> 00:01:16.740
as a way to open source
TensorFlow Extended, or TFX,

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:20.218
which was how TensorFlow is run
internally at Google.

00:01:20.218 --> 00:01:22.709
While it was originally just a simpler way

00:01:22.709 --> 00:01:26.201
to run TensorFlow jobs on Kubernetes,

00:01:26.201 --> 00:01:27.694
it has since expanded

00:01:27.694 --> 00:01:31.437
to become a multi-architecture,
multi-cloud framework

00:01:31.437 --> 00:01:34.169
for running entire
machine learning pipelines

00:01:34.169 --> 00:01:36.909
with many contributors,
all across the ecosystem.

00:01:37.379 --> 00:01:40.051
This might be a good time
for a little confession.

00:01:40.473 --> 00:01:43.779
I myself, I don't know
a whole lot about Kubernetes.

00:01:43.779 --> 00:01:46.641
Having spent the past several years
focused on TensorFlow

00:01:46.641 --> 00:01:48.531
and data science and machine learning,

00:01:48.531 --> 00:01:52.030
my Kubernetes skills are...
well, they're not exactly top notch.

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:56.949
So I'm really hoping that Kubeflow
can allow me to deploy TensorFlow

00:01:56.949 --> 00:02:00.471
and other machine workloads
onto Kubernetes successfully,

00:02:00.471 --> 00:02:04.098
because I do hear Kubernetes
is <i>pretty</i> great!

00:02:04.098 --> 00:02:07.318
So, let's see what it looks like
to use Kubeflow, shall we?

00:02:07.789 --> 00:02:12.829
Kubeflow aims to be able to run
anywhere Kubernetes can.

00:02:12.829 --> 00:02:16.041
This means everything from your laptop
to bare-metal servers

00:02:16.041 --> 00:02:17.890
all the way to the public cloud.

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:23.008
For now, let's check out how this works
on Google Kubernetes Engine, or GKE.

00:02:23.536 --> 00:02:27.195
Installing Kubeflow to GKE
is actually shockingly simple,

00:02:27.195 --> 00:02:30.239
since there's a click-to-deploy
user interface.

00:02:30.239 --> 00:02:34.020
All I had to do was enter
the project ID of my cloud project,

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:36.099
give my deployment a name,

00:02:36.099 --> 00:02:38.249
and choose a zone to put it in.

00:02:38.249 --> 00:02:42.719
Then, all the necessary resources
were provisioned automatically.

00:02:42.719 --> 00:02:45.272
Now <i>that</i> is my kind of deployment!

00:02:45.509 --> 00:02:48.478
So with Kubeflow installed,
and the UI accessible,

00:02:48.478 --> 00:02:51.580
you're ready to dive in
to the world of Kubeflow.

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:54.698
You can train models,
visualize them with TensorBoard,

00:02:54.698 --> 00:02:56.580
serve models, build pipelines,

00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:00.827
and log in to JupyterHub
to manage all these things.

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:05.209
Most of the time we get to use
the very same training code

00:03:05.209 --> 00:03:07.780
that we would be running locally anyway,

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:09.876
and just add some configuration files

00:03:09.876 --> 00:03:12.218
for the Kubeflow-specific parts,

00:03:12.218 --> 00:03:15.492
and then you can run them in Kubeflow
with no code changes.

00:03:15.789 --> 00:03:19.286
I can perform training
on my Kubernetes cluster

00:03:19.286 --> 00:03:20.610
using Kubeflow,

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:23.518
and have the exported model
either stay on disk

00:03:23.518 --> 00:03:26.269
in the Kubernetes cluster, or send it off

00:03:26.269 --> 00:03:28.251
to a Google Cloud Storage bucket,

00:03:28.251 --> 00:03:31.390
allowing me to easily share
or deploy the trained model

00:03:31.390 --> 00:03:33.415
to systems outside of the cluster.

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:36.133
Alright, that's enough of me talking.

00:03:36.133 --> 00:03:38.669
If you're interested in running
machine learning pipelines

00:03:38.669 --> 00:03:40.030
on Kubernetes,

00:03:40.030 --> 00:03:42.429
then it's time for you
to get started on Kubeflow.

00:03:42.429 --> 00:03:45.089
I've linked to a number
of codelabs and resources

00:03:45.089 --> 00:03:48.389
in the description down below
to help you get started.

00:03:48.389 --> 00:03:51.730
Thanks for watching this episode
of <i>Cloud AI Adventures,</i>

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:54.430
and if you enjoyed it,
be sure to hit that <i>like</i> button

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:57.150
and subscribe to get
all the latest updates

00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:58.794
right when they come out.

00:03:58.794 --> 00:04:01.190
For now, head down
to the description below

00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:04.200
for more information about
how to get Kubeflow up and running

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:05.929
on your Kubernetes cluster.

00:04:07.208 --> 00:04:09.629
♪ (music) ♪

